Q: How can I check whether a string contains only digits in Go? A: <p>This solution has the advantage to be clearer for a beginner point of view: it is a working example, with a direct link to the 'playground', that is the online runnable version of this code. Alternatively, you have to copy the code and run it on visual studio code or other IDE.</p>
<p>Two kind of solutions have been proposed elsewhere:</p>
<ol>
<li>the first considers the string as a whole, do not check every rune</li>
<li>the second checks rune by rune whether it is an unicode digit or not.</li>
</ol>
<p>Some solution lacks the ability to intercept the &quot;+&quot; and &quot;-&quot; sign as a non digit value, like &quot;-123&quot;.</p>
<p>This is a complete example of a 'check rune by rune' solution, inspired by a previous one and refined, and works well on the edge case &quot;-123&quot;.</p>
<p>online runnable code, the playground web console:
<a href="https://go.dev/play/p/f1Edhq4fA6z" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://go.dev/play/p/f1Edhq4fA6z</a></p>
<pre><code>package main

import (
    &quot;fmt&quot;
    &quot;unicode&quot;
)


func main() {


    OnlyDigits := func(str string) bool {
        if len(str) == 0 {
            return false
        }
        for _, r := range str {
            if !unicode.IsDigit(r) {
                return false // not an unicode digit -&gt; false
            }
        }
        return true
    }

    // usage:
    str1 := &quot;-123&quot;

    bool1 := OnlyDigits(str1)
    if !bool1 {
        fmt.Println(&quot;error, not only digits in&quot;, str1)
    } else {
        fmt.Println(&quot;ok, only digits in&quot;, str1)
    }
    // error, not only digits in -123

}
</code></pre>

Q: JSON Serializing java.sql.Time, java.sql.Date, and java.sql.Timestamp with minimal allocation A: <p>You can drop both <code>GregorianCalendar</code>s and get true zero-allocation extraction with pure integer math, the same epoch-day algorithm <code>java.time.LocalDate</code> uses internally (<a href="http://howardhinnant.github.io/date_algorithms.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Hinnant's <code>civil_from_days</code></a>):</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>private static void civilFromEpochDay(long epochDay, int[] ymd) {
    long z = epochDay + 719_468L;
    long era = Math.floorDiv(z, 146_097L);
    long doe = z - era * 146_097L;
    long yoe = (doe - doe/1460 + doe/36524 - doe/146096) / 365;
    long y   = yoe + era * 400;
    long doy = doe - (365*yoe + yoe/4 - yoe/100);
    long mp  = (5*doy + 2) / 153;
    ymd[2] = (int) (doy - (153*mp + 2)/5 + 1);          // day
    ymd[1] = (int) (mp + (mp &lt; 10 ? 3 : -9));           // month
    ymd[0] = (int) (y + (ymd[1] &lt;= 2 ? 1 : 0));         // year
}
</code></pre>
<p>Time-of-day is trivial integer division once you have ms-of-day (<code>s = msOfDay/1000, hour = s/3600, min = (s/60)%60, sec = s%60</code>).</p>
<p>Use <code>Math.floorDiv/floorMod</code> (not <code>//%</code>) to get <code>epochDay/millisOfDay</code> from <code>getTime()</code>, so pre-1970 dates still work.</p>
<p>For <code>Time</code>'s local-zone offset: <code>TimeZone.getDefault()</code> clones on every call, cache it once as a field, same as your calendars:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>private final TimeZone localZone = TimeZone.getDefault(); // fetch ONCE
</code></pre>
<p><code>localZone.getOffset(millis)</code> itself returns a primitive <code>int</code> with no allocation.
Add that offset to the raw millis before computing millis-of-day for <code>Time</code>, and you've eliminated the <code>Calendar</code>s entirely.</p>

Q: DocumentRoot and VSCode intelephense A: <p>Setting an absolute path is what has worked for me:</p>
<pre><code>{
    &quot;intelephense.environment.documentRoot&quot;: &quot;/home/&lt;user&gt;/Documents/Code/&lt;repoName&gt;/public&quot;
}
</code></pre>
<p>(and don't forget to restart / reload vscode)</p>
